<div id="features" class="features">
  <section class="feature" aria-labelledby="capture-title">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2 id="capture-title">Add a passage. Keep its source.</h2>
      <p>Select text in Safari, then press <kbd>⌃⇧H</kbd> on Mac or choose Twine from the page menu on iPhone. Auto-highlight on Mac saves new selections. Twine keeps the page title and source link.</p>
      <ul class="checks" role="list">
        <li>Saved highlights return when a page's text still matches</li>
        <li>Source links open at the saved passage</li>
        <li>Option-click to remove on Mac, or double-tap on iPhone</li>
        <li>Undo a removal from the page</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="media">
      {% shot "capture", "mac", "The training section of Wikipedia's large language model article in Safari, with the passage about model training costs highlighted in yellow.", "Safari on a public article with one or two saved Twine highlights. No transient messages or recording needed.", false, "Mac · A highlighted passage in Safari", 1960, 1280 %}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature feature-library" aria-labelledby="library-title">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2 id="library-title">Find what you kept.</h2>
      <p>Your highlights form one searchable library. Browse by website and page, search the words you remember, or narrow by date. Explore the included starter library, then add your own highlights. Copy passages as text or Markdown when you need them.</p>
      <ul class="checks" role="list">
        <li>Organized by website and page</li>
        <li>Search highlight text, page titles and websites</li>
        <li>A shared library on Mac and iPhone through your private iCloud</li>
        <li>Find highlights in Spotlight or open them from Shortcuts</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="media">
      {% shot "library-duo", "duo", "Twine on iPhone Duo with Wikipedia highlights on the left and the selected training-cost passage, source and actions on the right.", "iPhone Duo open in landscape orientation, with the library and selected highlight details visible side by side.", false, "iPhone Duo · Wikipedia highlights and the selected passage", 2000, 1407 %}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature" aria-labelledby="ai-title">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2 id="ai-title">Let AI tools search your saved passages.</h2>
      <p>With Twine Pro, turn on AI Access and keep Twine running on your Mac. Connect a compatible AI tool to search your library and read saved passages with their source links. The connection is read-only.</p>
      <ul class="prompts" role="list" aria-label="Example requests">
        <li>Find my highlights about onboarding and group them by theme.</li>
        <li>Compare what my saved sources say about remote work, with links to each page.</li>
        <li>Use my highlights from the past week to outline a research brief, and list the excerpts you used.</li>
      </ul>
      <ul class="checks" role="list">
        <li>Off until you turn it on, and reachable only from your Mac</li>
        <li>Protected by an access token you can replace at any time</li>
        <li>Tools can search, read and export highlights, but can't change them through this connection</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">Example requests for a connected AI tool, not a built-in Twine chat. Responses depend on your tool and the highlights you've saved.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="media media-settings">
      {% shot "ai-access", "window", "Twine's AI settings on a Mac with AI Access enabled, a local server URL and controls to copy connection settings or regenerate the token; no token value is displayed.", "Twine Settings > AI > AI Access, with the local server enabled and connection controls visible. Keep access tokens concealed; no chat response needed.", false, "Mac · Read-only access for connected AI tools", 1040, 1436 %}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature" aria-labelledby="context-title">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2 id="context-title">Keep the passage in context.</h2>
      <p>Twine Pro's on-device AI context can explain how a highlight fits into the surrounding text. Read it under <strong>Background</strong> in the highlight's details. The explanation stays with the highlight, but isn't included in AI Access.</p>
      <ul class="checks" role="list">
        <li>Off until you turn it on</li>
        <li>Saved with the highlight and synced to your other devices</li>
        <li>Writing or refreshing an explanation needs Apple Intelligence</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="media">
      {% shot "context", "phone", "An iPhone showing the highlighted Wikipedia training-cost passage, its Background explanation, the Refresh control and the source.", "An iPhone highlight detail with a completed AI context explanation. Use a public passage with enough surrounding text; avoid loading or unavailable states.", false, "iPhone · The passage and its on-device explanation", 1206, 2622 %}
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

<section class="band" aria-labelledby="privacy-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 id="privacy-title">Your library stays yours.</h2>
    <div>
      <ul class="checks" role="list">
        <li>No Twine account to create</li>
        <li>Highlights live on your devices and sync through your private iCloud. The developer never receives them.</li>
        <li>No analytics, ads or trackers, in the app or on this site.</li>
        <li>AI Access is off until you turn it on. The connection stays on your Mac; a tool you connect handles what it reads under its own privacy policy.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="more"><a class="text-link" href="/privacy">Read the privacy policy</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

